{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% import "macros.html.j2" as m %}
{% block content %}
<h1>{{ page.title }}</h1>
<p class="lede">Which words and phrases are over-represented in talk abstracts compared with posters? Each phrase gets
an odds ratio: how much more likely an abstract containing it is to be a talk.</p>
<p>Phrases are 1–3-word sequences from the abstract text. Variants of one idea that turn up in mostly the same
abstracts (“massively parallel reporter”, “parallel reporter assay”, “MPRA”) are grouped under the
best-scoring one, with the rest listed beneath it in small type.</p>
<nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#by-year">Phrases overrepresented in talks</a>
{% if drivers_json %}<a href="#explorer">Look up a phrase</a>{% endif %}
{% if "trends_phrases" in trends %}<a href="#phrase-trends">Phrase trends</a>{% endif %}
{% if "trends_biobanks" in trends %}<a href="#biobanks">Biobanks</a>{% endif %}
{% if "trends_shift" in trends %}<a href="#shift">What changed in {{ years[-1].year }}</a>{% endif %}
{% if "trends_sample_size" in trends %}<a href="#sample-size">Sample size</a>{% endif %}
{% if words %}<a href="#words">Words A to Z</a>{% endif %}
{% if "trends_words_enriched" in trends %}<a href="#words-enriched">Words more common in {{ years[-1].year }}</a>{% endif %}
{% if "trends_genes" in trends %}<a href="#genes">Genes</a>{% endif %}
{% if score %}<a href="#score">Score your text</a>{% endif %}</nav>
<span id="by-year"></span>
{{ m.toolbar(years, pooled) }}
{% if pooled %}
{% set p = pooled %}
<section data-show-year="all">
<h2>All years <span class="sub">· {{ p.label }}</span></h2>
<p>{{ "{:,}".format(p.summary.abstracts) }} abstracts, {{ "{:,}".format(p.summary.talks) }} of them talks: a pooled
talk rate of <strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(100 * p.summary.talk_rate) }}%</strong>. Odds ratios are combined across years
(Mantel–Haenszel, stratified by year), so a phrase that rose or fell over time isn't credited with each year's
different baseline. {{ "{:,}".format(p.summary.truncated) }} abstracts cut off in a booklet are left out of the phrase
counts.</p>
{{ m.figure("img/all_headline.png", "Dot plot of the phrases most over-represented in ASHG %s talks versus posters, with odds ratios and 95%% confidence intervals." % p.label) }}
{% call m.details("Top 30 phrases each way") %}
<div class="cols">
{% for dir, label in [("talk", "More common in talks"), ("poster", "More common in posters")] %}
<div class="scroll"><p class="table-title"><span class="key" style="background: var(--{{ dir }})"></span>{{ label }}</p>
{{ p.premium[dir] }}</div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
{% endcall %}
</section>
{% endif %}
{% for y in years %}
<section data-show-year="{{ y.year }}">
{{ m.year_heading(y) }}
<p>{{ "{:,}".format(y.summary.abstracts) }} abstracts, {{ "{:,}".format(y.summary.talks) }} of them talks: a baseline
talk rate of <strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(100 * y.summary.talk_rate) }}%</strong>.
{% if y.summary.truncated %}{{ y.summary.truncated }} abstracts are cut off mid-text in the booklet itself; they are
left out of the phrase counts.{% endif %}</p>
{{ m.figure("img/%d_headline.png" % y.year, "Dot plot of the phrases most over-represented in ASHG %d talks versus posters, with odds ratios and 95%% confidence intervals." % y.year,
            "The panel at the bottom compares the largest sample size stated in each abstract (e.g. “12,000 individuals”, “N = 500 patients”).") }}
<div class="cols">
{% for dir, label in [("talk", "More common in talks"), ("poster", "More common in posters")] %}
<div class="scroll"><p class="table-title"><span class="key" style="background: var(--{{ dir }})"></span>{{ label }}</p>
{{ y.premium[dir] }}</div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
</section>
{% endfor %}

{% if drivers_json %}
{% call m.explorer(drivers_json) %}
<p>The phrases in the figures below (trends, biobanks, what changed in {{ years[-1].year }}) plus the top 50 each way
across all years. See how often talks and posters mention a phrase
each year, then which first-author institutions and metro areas write about it (among those with at least one
talk mentioning it; an abstract counts for each of its institutions). Hover or focus a row for that institution's
own mention rate and odds of a talk. Searching also finds every other phrase in at least 5 talks over all years; for those the counts
are exact matches in the abstract text (no titles or plurals), and there is no institution breakdown.</p>
{% endcall %}
{% endif %}

{% if "trends_phrases" in trends %}
<h2 id="phrase-trends">Phrase trends</h2>
<p>Year by year, {{ years[0].year }}–{{ years[-1].year }}: the share of talks and of posters mentioning selected phrases.
For any other phrase, and who writes about it, <a href="#explorer">look it up</a> above.</p>
{{ m.figure("img/trends_phrases.png", "For selected phrases, the share of talks and of posters mentioning each one, per year.") }}
{% endif %}

{% if "trends_biobanks" in trends %}
<h2 id="biobanks">Biobanks</h2>
<p>How often abstracts mention the most-cited biobanks, {{ years[0].year }}–{{ years[-1].year }}, counting common
acronyms (UKB, MVP, BBJ). UK Biobank is in about one abstract in eight every year; All of Us grew six-fold. To compare
talks with posters for any of them, <a href="#explorer">look it up</a> above.</p>
{{ m.figure("img/trends_biobanks.png", "Share of accepted abstracts mentioning each of eight biobanks per year, with the number of abstracts mentioning it.") }}
{% endif %}

{% if "trends_shift" in trends %}
<h2 id="shift">What changed in {{ years[-1].year }}</h2>
{% macro lookup(phrase, shown=None) %}<a href="phrases.html?phrase={{ phrase | urlencode }}#explorer">{{ shown or phrase }}</a>{% endmacro %}
<p>Clinical vocabulary stopped leaning toward posters. “{{ lookup("patients", "Patients") }}”, “{{ lookup("likely pathogenic") }}”
and “{{ lookup("next-generation sequencing") }}” leaned toward posters over {{ years[0].year }}–{{ years[-2].year }} (pooled
odds ratios 0.4–0.7); in {{ years[-1].year }} all three were at least as common in talks. Scanning every phrase for the
largest {{ years[-1].year }} shifts turns up more of the same: “{{ lookup("idiopathic") }}”, “{{ lookup("genetic disease") }}”
and “{{ lookup("therapy") }}”. Click one to look it up.</p>
<p class="caveat"><strong>Suggestive, not established.</strong> The top row was chosen before looking at
{{ years[-1].year }}; the bottom row was picked for having the largest shifts, so its p-values overstate the
evidence. Across all ~10,800 phrases tested, no single shift survives a false-discovery-rate correction.</p>
{{ m.figure("img/trends_shift.png", "Share of talks and of posters mentioning six clinical terms per year, with the odds ratio of a talk before and in %s and the p-value for the change." % years[-1].year) }}
{% endif %}

{% if "trends_sample_size" in trends %}
<h2 id="sample-size">Sample size</h2>
<p>Talks report larger cohorts than posters, every year: the largest human-subject sample size
each abstract states (“12,000 individuals”, “N = 450”); the bars span the middle half of abstracts that state one.</p>
{{ m.figure("img/trends_sample_size.png", "Median largest stated sample size per abstract, with interquartile bars, and the share of abstracts stating a sample size, for talks and posters each year.") }}
{% endif %}

{% if words %}
<h2 id="words">Words, A to Z</h2>
<p>Every word in at least 10 abstracts, in alphabetical order, by the share of abstracts using it. Common English
words (“the”, “of”) are left out; forms of one word (“remain”, “remains”) sit side by side and form towers, and the
top of each tower is labelled. Hover a point for its word. The year buttons at the top of the page switch years; words
keep their place and the scale is the same every year.</p>
{% if manhattan_src %}
<div class="mh" data-manhattan="words" data-src="{{ manhattan_src }}" data-label="Manhattan-style plot: share of abstracts using each word, words in alphabetical order.">
<p class="mh-title"></p>
<img class="mh-fallback" src="img/all_words.png" alt="Share of all abstracts using each word, words in alphabetical order, {{ pooled.label }} pooled." loading="lazy">
</div>
{% endif %}
<p class="table-note">Images to download: {% if "all_words.png" in words %}<a href="img/all_words.png">all years</a>{% endif %}
{% for y in years %}{% if "%d_words.png" % y.year in words %} · <a href="img/{{ y.year }}_words.png">{{ y.year }}</a>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</p>
{% endif %}

{% if "trends_words_enriched" in trends %}
<h3 id="words-enriched">Words more common in {{ years[-1].year }}</h3>
<p>The same words, now by how strongly each is overrepresented in {{ years[-1].year }} abstracts compared with
{{ years[0].year }}–{{ years[-2].year }} (one-sided Fisher's exact test per word; higher is stronger). Most of the
strongest are general phrasing rather than new topics: “framework” is in 27% of {{ years[-1].year }} abstracts against
10% before, and “remains”, “incompletely”, “supporting” and “consistent” rose too. One new topic stands out:
“AlphaGenome”, in 31 abstracts, none before. Hover a point for its word; click it to look it up in the table below.</p>
{% if manhattan_src %}
<div class="mh" data-manhattan="enriched" data-src="{{ manhattan_src }}" data-label="Manhattan-style plot of every word, alphabetically, by minus log10 p for being more common in {{ years[-1].year }} abstracts than in earlier years, with a Bonferroni line.">
<img class="mh-fallback" src="img/trends_words_enriched.png" alt="Manhattan-style plot of every word, alphabetically, by minus log10 p for being more common in {{ years[-1].year }} abstracts than in earlier years." loading="lazy">
</div>
<p class="table-note">−log₁₀ p from a one-sided Fisher's exact test per word; dashed line: Bonferroni correction over
every word at 5%. <a href="img/trends_words_enriched.png">Image to download</a></p>
{% else %}
{{ m.figure("img/trends_words_enriched.png", "Manhattan-style plot of every word, alphabetically, by minus log10 p for being more common in %d abstracts than in earlier years, with a Bonferroni line." % years[-1].year) }}
{% endif %}
{% if words_enriched_json %}
{{ m.words_table(years, words_enriched_json) }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if "trends_genes" in trends %}
<h2 id="genes">Genes</h2>
<p>How often each protein-coding gene is named, placed along the genome (hg38). APOE leads every year, with BRCA1,
BRCA2 and TP53 next. Gene symbols that in abstracts mostly mean something else (MAF for minor allele frequency, HR for
hazard ratio, TF for transcription factor, CAD for coronary artery disease) are left out. Hover a point for its gene;
the year buttons at the top of the page switch years.</p>
{% if manhattan_src %}
<div class="mh" data-manhattan="genes" data-src="{{ manhattan_src }}" data-label="Manhattan-style plot: share of abstracts naming each protein-coding gene, along the genome.">
<p class="mh-title"></p>
<img class="mh-fallback" src="img/trends_genes.png" alt="Manhattan-style plot, one row per year: share of abstracts naming each protein-coding gene, along the genome." loading="lazy">
</div>
<p class="table-note"><a href="img/trends_genes.png">Image to download</a> (every year, one row each)</p>
{% else %}
{{ m.figure("img/trends_genes.png", "Manhattan-style plot, one row per year: share of abstracts naming each protein-coding gene, along the genome, with the most-mentioned genes labelled.") }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if score %}
<h2 id="score">Score your text</h2>
{% set c = score.check %}
<p>Paste an abstract to count how many of the {{ score.groups | length }} phrases overrepresented in ASHG
talks it uses, and where that count falls among the {{ "{:,}".format(score.n_abstracts) }} abstracts accepted
{{ score.years[0] }}–{{ score.years[1] }}.</p>
<p class="caveat"><strong>Your text stays on your device.</strong> It is scored by this page's own script as you type;
nothing is uploaded or saved. Only a rounded percentile is shown, from counts of abstracts per score.</p>

<section class="scorer">
<label for="sc-text" class="lbl">Abstract text</label>
<textarea id="sc-text" rows="10" spellcheck="false" placeholder="Paste the abstract text here (the body; the title is optional)."></textarea>
<div class="filters">
  <label class="lbl">Or open a text file <input type="file" id="sc-file" accept=".txt,text/plain"></label>
  <span class="seg"><button type="button" id="sc-clear">Clear</button></span>
</div>
<div id="sc-result" hidden aria-live="polite">
  <p class="sc-headline"><strong id="sc-score"></strong> overrepresented phrases: more than about
  <strong id="sc-pct"></strong> of accepted abstracts.</p>
  <p id="sc-short" class="empty" hidden>That is <span id="sc-words"></span> words; abstracts run about 300–350, so a
  shorter text scores lower.</p>
  <h4>Phrases found</h4>
  <p class="table-note">Strongest overrepresentation first; hover one for its odds ratio.</p>
  <div id="sc-found" class="chips"></div>
  <p id="sc-none" class="empty" hidden>None of the phrases.</p>
</div>
<h4>How talks and posters score</h4>
<div class="legend"><span><i style="background: var(--talk)"></i>share of talks</span><span><i style="background: var(--poster)"></i>share of posters</span></div>
<svg id="sc-chart" role="img" viewBox="0 0 560 190" aria-label="Histogram: share of talks and of posters by number of overrepresented phrases used"></svg>
<p class="table-note">Median talk {{ "%g" | format(score.median.talks) }}, median poster
{{ "%g" | format(score.median.posters) }}. Hover a score for its counts.</p>
<script type="application/json" id="sc-data">{{ score.json }}</script>
</section>

<h3>What the score is</h3>
<p>The phrases are the 1–3-word phrases more common in talk abstracts than in poster abstracts over
{{ score.years[0] }}–{{ score.years[1] }}, significant after a Bonferroni correction across all
{{ "{:,}".format(score.n_tested) }} phrases tested (z ≥ {{ score.z }}). Variants of one idea found in mostly the same
abstracts (“massively parallel reporter”, “MPRA”) count once. The score is how many of them a text uses, the same way
for your text as for every accepted abstract.</p>
<p>Mostly they mark topics (functional genomics, single-cell and chromatin work, very large cohorts) and the way
large studies describe their results (“largest”, “novel”, “million”), more than style.</p>

<h3>How well it separates talks and posters</h3>
<p>To check the score on abstracts it was not built from, the phrase list was rebuilt from {{ c.train[0] }}–{{ c.train[1] }}
alone ({{ c.groups }} phrases) and used to score the {{ "{:,}".format(c.talks + c.posters) }} abstracts of {{ c.test }}.
A random {{ c.test }} talk outscores a random {{ c.test }} poster <strong>{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * c.auc) }}%</strong>
of the time (50% would be no better than a coin flip; ties count half). For comparison, ranking abstracts by length
alone (how many different words they use) puts the talk ahead {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * c.auc_length) }}% of the time,
so the phrase count reflects more than talk abstracts running a little longer.</p>
<p class="caveat"><strong>A description, not a verdict.</strong> Talks and posters overlap heavily at every score.
Accepted abstracts only, and authors can ask for a poster, so this is association, not acceptance odds. Adding phrases
to an abstract will raise its score without changing the work.</p>

{% call m.details("All %d phrases" % (score.groups | length)) %}
<p class="table-note">Talks and posters using each phrase in their text, and its odds ratio of a talk, combined across
years. Variants listed under a phrase count toward the score too.</p>
<div class="scroll">{{ score.table }}</div>
{% endcall %}
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
